R['notes'].extend([
{'id':'N07','text':'Root targeted set-theoretic check of a), conditional on interpreting Sigma_01 as a relation in Sigma_0 times Sigma_1, and similarly the other Sigma_ij, as indicated by the later triple-product formula and diagram. The literal opening self-inclusion does not itself supply that hypothesis. On the disjoint union, adjoin only identities within each level and the indicated relations between increasing levels. Reflexivity then follows from the adjoined identities, and antisymmetry follows from strictly increasing grades for every nonidentity pair. The only possible composition of two nonidentity pairs is 0→1→2; its transitivity requirement is exactly a), the projection of Sigma_012 to (x0,x2) lying in Sigma_02.'},
{'id':'N08','text':'Root targeted injectivity check, under the same relational/product interpretation and a) together with the injection hypotheses in b). Given (x1,x2), the injection Sigma_01→Sigma_1 determines x0, so Sigma_012→Sigma_12 is injective. Given (x0,x2), the injection Sigma_12→Sigma_2 determines x1, so Sigma_012→Sigma_02 is injective. Composing the latter map with the assumed injection Sigma_02→Sigma_2 gives injectivity of Sigma_012→Sigma_2. Thus the overworked target in the continued b) line cannot be decided between 02 and 2 by these mathematical consequences.'},
{'id':'N09','text':'Root targeted set-theoretic check of the upper-square description, conditional on the same relational/product interpretation and the b) identifications of Sigma_02 and Sigma_12 as subsets of Sigma_2. Their set-theoretic fibre product over Sigma_2 is their intersection. The upper square is Cartesian as a square of sets exactly when its canonical Sigma_012 map identifies Sigma_012 with that intersection, as c) demands. This checks the formulation; it does not derive c) from a) and b), establish topological pullback identifications, or verify bordant, smooth, transversality, or fibration claims. The literal dans Sigma_12 remains unchanged.'}
])
